One change to tweetnacl.c (sha256 here:
6c88d4e8c75200cb9a236bd9c8d6ab03172ed8484628032a6e172875cee31620). Two
left shifts of a signed carry that can be negative, which is undefined
behaviour in C11 and which UBSan reports in the host tests, became the
equivalent multiplications:

    car25519: o[i]-=c<<16;          ->  o[i]-=c*65536;
    modL:     x[j] -= carry << 8;   ->  x[j] -= carry * 256;

A multiplication by 2^16 or 2^8 is what the shift was written to compute,
negative carries included, and it cannot overflow here (the carries are far
below 2^47). The RFC 8032 vectors in tt7d/test_bundle.c pass before and after.

Use: tt7d/sign.c includes tweetnacl.c and calls only crypto_sign_open (ed25519
verify, with TweetNaCl's own SHA-512). It silences two warnings for this file
only (-Wsign-compare, -Wunterminated-string-initialization), which both gcc and
zig cc raise on this source. TweetNaCl needs a `randombytes` symbol for
key generation, which tt7d never does; sign.c supplies one that aborts.

Why TweetNaCl and not Monocypher: one file, about 800 lines, public domain, and
it already includes the SHA-512 that ed25519 needs. Monocypher is faster, but
as far as we know (not re-checked on 2026-09-28) its default signature is
EdDSA over BLAKE2b, with RFC 8032 ed25519 in a separate optional file. Speed
does not matter for one signature check per update.
